.Dd November 10, 2026
.Dt VGEM 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm vgem
.Nd virtual GEM provider, buffers and fences with no hardware
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Cm rach2.vgem
.Pp
.In drm/drm.h
.In drm/vgem_drm.h
.Pp
.Pa /dev/dri/cardN
.Pa /dev/dri/renderDN
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
driver is a device of buffers in memory with no hardware behind it, as
Linux 6.12's vgem is.
A program makes its buffers, exports them through PRIME to another
driver, and holds them busy with fences it signals itself, which the
importer waits for as it would for a device's work.
IGT's tests use it so to hold back the work they give a GPU, and to fill a
GPU's ring with work that cannot run.
It starts only when
.Cm rach2.vgem
is on the kernel command line, after the other display drivers, and binds
one device; Linux loads its module on demand, as IGT does with
.Ql modprobe vgem .
.Pp
The device is a DRM device, as
.Xr drm 4
describes, registered as
.Ql vgem
with Linux's identity: description
.Ql Virtual GEM provider ,
version 1.0.0 and date 20120112, on the platform bus as
.Pa /sys/devices/platform/vgem ,
whose GET_UNIQUE name is
.Ql vgem .
It has a card node and a render node, and the features
.Dv DRIVER_GEM
and
.Dv DRIVER_RENDER .
Its buffers are dumb buffers in memory, mapped write-combined, since it
has no call that would bracket a processor's access for an importer.
The kernel logs
.Dq vgem: registered
with the card.
.Ss Fences
A fence of
.Nm
signals when its program signals it, when the program's file closes, or
10 seconds after it was made, whichever comes first, as Linux's.
Each is on a timeline of its own, which
.Dv SYNC_IOC_FILE_INFO
names
.Ql unbound ,
and an open file numbers its fences from 1, never giving a number twice.
.Sh IOCTLS
Besides those of
.Xr drm 4 ,
on either node:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_VGEM_FENCE_ATTACH Fa "struct drm_vgem_fence_attach *"
.Bd -literal
struct drm_vgem_fence_attach {
	__u32 handle;
	__u32 flags;
	__u32 out_fence;
	__u32 pad;
};
.Ed
.Pp
Makes a fence on the buffer
.Fa handle
and returns its number in
.Fa out_fence .
With
.Dv VGEM_FENCE_WRITE
in
.Fa flags
the fence is the buffer's writer, which every importer's access waits
for; without it, a reader, which an importer's write waits for.
.It Dv DRM_IOCTL_VGEM_FENCE_SIGNAL Fa "struct drm_vgem_fence_signal *"
.Bd -literal
struct drm_vgem_fence_signal {
	__u32 fence;
	__u32 flags;
};
.Ed
.Pp
Signals the fence numbered
.Fa fence
and gives its number up;
.Fa flags
is 0.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /dev/dri/renderDN
.It Pa /dev/dri/cardN
The card node of the device.
.It Pa /dev/dri/renderDN
Its render node.
.El
.Sh ERRORS
Besides those of
.Xr drm 4 :
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Er EBUSY
FENCE_ATTACH of a writer to a buffer with a fence pending, or of a reader
to one with a writer pending.
.It Er EINVAL
FENCE_ATTACH with flags other than
.Dv VGEM_FENCE_WRITE
or a pad that is not 0; FENCE_SIGNAL with flags.
.It Er ENOENT
FENCE_ATTACH of a handle the file does not have; FENCE_SIGNAL of a number
the file was never given or has given up.
.It Er ETIMEDOUT
FENCE_SIGNAL of a fence that had signalled after its 10 seconds; the
number is given up.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr drm 4 ,
.Xr i915 4 ,
.Xr vkms 4 ,
.Xr dma-buf 7
.Sh HISTORY
The driver follows Linux 6.12's vgem: its identity, its shmem buffers and
its two fence calls with their errors and timeout.
